// What a Khayyam-written compiler needs fourth: repetition. A reader reads one
// declaration at a time until the unit ends, and a body's four commands carry no
// form that repeats one of them — the grammar ships no control-flow keyword, and
// the Control Flow protocol builds looping as a library over the realization's
// underlying primitives
// (docs/protocols/process/control-flow.md → The Preferred Form: Domain-Specific
// Conditional Methods: "a realization can provide low-level compiler-intrinsic
// jump methods for repetition and use its collection-iteration contract").
//
// So the question is not whether a loop exists in the grammar — it does not, and
// that is settled — but whether a program can be written to drive one.

tp Text cp {
}

tp Cursor cp {
}

tp Reader cp {
}

tp Next mt (self Reader) (cursor Cursor) (more Text) {
    tp KeepGoing sc {
    }
}
